(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609109400-kotoba-form-dag-kotoba-and-what-guest-and-host-actually-name")
  (:adr/topic "kotoba-form-naming-and-the-authority-axis")
  (:adr/title "Kotoba Form, DAG-Kotoba as a profile rather than a codec, and what guest and host actually name")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang" "kotoba-lang/kototama" "kotoba-lang/amu"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609107000-everything-is-a-form-arranged-against-what-runs"
                "adr-2609106000-the-kir-block-shape-is-an-ipld-schema-and-what-it-cannot-pin"
                "adr-2609092600-app-plane-identity-naming-location-kind"
                "adr-2609076000-kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

Owner decision 2026-09-10, on the naming for the cross-plane object and on
whether the guest/host split still says the right thing under it.

## Decision 1: the layers, and one name per layer

    Kotoba          the ecosystem and the language philosophy
    Kotoba ADL      the human- and agent-facing pure S authoring language
    Kotoba Form     the universal semantic object
    Kotoba Schema   the type and constraint system over Forms
    DAG-Kotoba      the canonical IPLD representation profile
    DAG-CBOR        the canonical binary codec
    CID             object identity

    foo.kotoba -> parse -> Kotoba ADL -> schema check / desugar
      -> Kotoba Schema-typed Forms -> canonicalize -> DAG-Kotoba
      -> DAG-CBOR -> CID

## Decision 2: DAG-Kotoba is a PROFILE, not a codec

In IPLD practice `DAG-*` reads as a codec name -- DAG-CBOR, DAG-JSON, DAG-PB.
The canonical bytes here ARE DAG-CBOR, so calling the layer above it
DAG-Kotoba without saying which is which invites a reader to think there are
two codecs and to ask which one produced a block.

    DAG-Kotoba = an IPLD Data Model profile: Kotoba Forms on the Data Model,
                 with canonical link and schema semantics
    DAG-CBOR   = the codec that turns that into bytes

One codec. The profile constrains what may appear; the codec decides the
bytes. This is the same separation ADR-2609076000 decision 5 draws inside the
DefCID: the logical form is sealed and the representation is not.

## Decision 3: KDN is not the name

Rejected. `KDN` sits in the series JSON / EDN / KDN and therefore reads as a
data NOTATION, which boxes the thing at a size it is not. The scope is code,
query, schema, policy, plan, receipt and patch, and a notation name makes
every one of those look like a document format.

## Decision 4: `Kotoba Form` collides with two live uses, and the boundary is
stated rather than assumed

Measured 2026-09-10, both confirmed by reading the repositories:

- `kotoba-lang/forms` is an office product -- *Portable CLJC model for
  kotoba-lang/forms*, with a Pages editor. Unrelated: it means a form somebody
  fills in. It keeps its name and its README says which sense it is.
- `kotoba-sema` describes its pipeline as `source bytes -> forms -> semantic
  analysis -> checked kotoba-hir`, and its own first line is *reading forms*.
  This one is NOT a collision, it is the same lineage at an earlier stage: a
  lowercase `form` is what the reader produces, and a `Kotoba Form` is what
  one becomes once it is schema-typed and content-addressed. The relation is
  refinement, and writing it down is what keeps the two from being read as
  rivals.

Per the workspace rule for a name that does not announce its own function, the
term is registered in `manifest/concept-vocabulary.edn` and the boundary above
is what the entry says.

## Decision 5: guest and host name a LOCATION, and the axis that matters is
AUTHORITY

The redesign the owner asked for. What `guest`/`host` encodes today is the
WASM embedding metaphor -- the host embeds and answers, the guest is embedded
and asks -- and that single word is carrying three different things at once:

  1. AUTHORITY   who must declare capability imports, and who answers them
  2. LOCATION    who is embedded in whom at run time
  3. TRUST       whose code is checked, and whose is relied on

The layering above exists to keep identity, naming and location apart
(ADR-2609092600), and location is configuration rather than design. A word
that binds authority to location is the same mistake one layer up.

**The vocabulary already leaks this, in the authority file itself.**
`lang/guest-grammar.edn` says, in its own scope key: *the historical key name
guest-grammar does not make .kotoba guest-only; provider components use the
same admitted grammar plus declared imports.* A provider is a guest by grammar
and a host by role. One word cannot carry both, and this note is the record of
it not doing so.

`kototama` reaches the same place from the other side: *Solo5 called this role
a tender. That word is a nautical metaphor for the attendant host. It is not
the product name.*

So:

- **AUTHORITY is the axis.** A Form DECLARES the capabilities it needs; a
  provider ANSWERS them. Declaring and answering are roles a component takes
  per capability, not a class it belongs to, and the same component can do
  both. `:ambient-authority-forbidden` and
  `:native-functionality-crosses :declared-capability-import` already state
  the rule; what changes is that the ROLE NAMES follow the rule instead of
  the embedding.
- **`host` keeps its narrow, true meaning: the runtime embedding.** Which
  process loads the artifact is a Location fact, with the same status as
  which hostname serves a block. It is configuration.
- **`guest` retires as a SEMANTIC term.** What it actually names is the
  least-authority profile, which is what `guest-grammar.edn`'s scope key has
  been saying in prose. New writing says *least-authority profile* or names
  the capability role directly.

## What is NOT done, deliberately

**No bulk rename.** Measured 2026-09-10 in `kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang/lang`
alone: `host` 773 occurrences, `guest` 234, `guest-grammar` 82. The workspace
rule is that a naming decision binds what is written next, not what exists --
and this file is one where a regular-expression rewrite would land inside
docstrings and prose that are describing the WASM embedding correctly.
`guest-grammar.edn` keeps its file name for the same reason `osaho` kept
`kotoba.kir.*`: names are not identity.

**No gate.** Nothing checks that new writing uses the authority vocabulary,
and this ADR does not invent one.

**The pipeline in decision 1 is a naming decision, not a claim that it runs.**
`Kotoba Schema` as a written artifact exists for KIR only, and only as of
today (`osaho/resources/kotoba/kir/definition-identity.ipldsch`,
ADR-2609106000). DAG-Kotoba as a named profile has no document yet.

## Reproduce

    git -C orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang show origin/main:lang/guest-grammar.edn \\
      | grep -A3 'guest-grammar/scope'
    git -C orgs/kotoba-lang/kototama show <remote>/main:README.md | head -12
    git -C orgs/kotoba-lang/forms show <remote>/main:README.md | head -8

The first prints the scope key that says the name is historical. The second
prints kototama refusing the tender metaphor as a product name. The third
prints the office product whose `forms` means something else.")))
